<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akapana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Akapana is the largest structure at Tiwanaku, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>: a terraced platform mound about 17 metres high, faced with stone and built mainly between about 500 and 950 CE. Its summit held buildings around a sunken court, and an internal drainage system carried water down through its terraces, probably as part of rituals linking it to sacred mountains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akapana.jpg" alt="Akapana" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vale Velard · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arqueologia_impresionante.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
